readme.migrations -- the SQL that creates the tables
====================================================

Every table Services keep in PostgreSQL is created by a migration: a pair
of SQL files, one that applies a change to the schema and one that undoes
it.  The core ships its own set; a module that needs tables ships its
set beside its code.  The build compiles the SQL into the executable or
the module, Services check it when the module is loaded, and it is applied
either automatically or by an operator with OperServ MIGRATION.  The code
is a port of ircu2's (doc/readme.migrations in ircu2), adapted to Services.

    include/migration.h            the rules and the API
    src/migration.c                the validator
    src/migration_run.c            the runner and the OperServ commands
    src/migrations/                the core's own set
    cmake/ServicesMigrations.cmake,
    cmake/GenerateMigrations.cmake the build side

1. Who applies what
-------------------

There are three kinds of migration, and they differ only in who decides
when they run.

- The core's set (src/migrations/, recorded under the module name "core")
  is applied when Services start, before any module is loaded.  It creates
  the table every migration is recorded in (`migrations'), the claim on
  the schema (`instance') and the list of Redis keys to correct
  (`store_stale').  No module may be called "core".

- The modules that keep Services' own data declare MODULE_MIGRATIONS_AUTO:
  in this release nickserv/main (nicknames, nickname groups, access lists,
  memos) and chanserv/main (channels, access lists, autokicks).  Their
  pending migrations are applied when the module is loaded, before
  init_module(): without their tables there would not even be an
  identified operator to apply them by hand.  A module whose migrations
  cannot be applied does not load, and when it is one of these, Services
  do not start.

- Every other module follows ircu2's rule: ITS MIGRATIONS NEVER RUN BY
  THEMSELVES.  Not when the module is loaded, not on REHASH, not on the
  first query.  Loading the module tells Services what SQL exists, and the
  log says what is pending:

      migration: module misc/scores has 2 pending migrations; apply with
      OperServ MIGRATION APPLY misc/scores

  Whether the database should run it, and when, is an operational decision
  on a live network, and it stays with the operator accountable for it.

2. What a module ships
----------------------

A module with migrations is a module built from a directory (see
BUILDING.md), with a migrations/ subdirectory:

    modules/<type>/<name>/
        <name>.c
        migrations/
            v1_create_scores.up.sql
            v1_create_scores.down.sql
            v2_add_rank.up.sql
            v2_add_rank.down.sql

The rules, all checked when the module is loaded:

- The file name is v<N>_<name>.up.sql or v<N>_<name>.down.sql, and nothing
  else.  Any other file in migrations/ is an error, not something ignored.
- <name> is letters, digits and underscores, at most 64 of them.
- <N> starts at 1 and runs without gaps or repeats; "v01" is refused.
- Every up has a down with the same version and the same name.

A migration nobody can undo is a migration nobody can safely apply to a
live network.  If the revert really is "nothing to undo", write a down
that says so; the core's v1 does, and its file explains why.

A module that breaks a rule does not load, and the log says which file is
wrong:

    modules: Could not load misc/scores: migrations/v2_add_rank has no
    down file; every up needs its down and every down needs its up

3. The SQL is not installed
---------------------------

The .sql files are compiled into the module (or, for the core's set, the
executable) as string literals, and never copied anywhere: not into the
build tree, not into the install tree.  A module carries its migrations
wherever it is installed, and the SQL beside a module cannot be a
different version from the code inside it, because there is no SQL beside
the module.

The build embeds whatever it finds and checks nothing; the rules are
enforced at load time, so that a malformed migration is an error the
operator reads.  A new .sql file in an existing migrations/ directory is
picked up by the next build.  A migrations/ directory added to a module
that had none needs CMake to run again (cmake -S . -B build) before the
build notices it.

4. OperServ MIGRATION
---------------------

    MIGRATION LIST
        Every row of the migrations table, every module at once: the record
        of what has actually been applied to this database.

    MIGRATION STATUS <module>
        What the module ships, and which of it is applied:

            Migrations for misc/scores:
              v1_create_scores  applied
              v2_add_rank  pending
            End of migrations for misc/scores (2 declared)

        "core" shows the core's set.

    MIGRATION APPLY <module> [v<N>]
        Applies every pending migration in order, stopping at the first
        that fails; with a version, up to and including it.

    MIGRATION REVERT <module> [v<N>]
        Reverts ONE migration: the newest applied.  With a version, reverts
        down to and including it, newest first.

APPLY and REVERT are deliberately asymmetric.  Going forward is the
expected operation; going back destroys whatever the migration built, and
"all of them" is not something to do by leaving an argument off.

LIST and STATUS are limited to Services admins; APPLY and REVERT to the
Services super-user (ServicesRoot).  The module must be loaded.  Neither
APPLY nor REVERT accepts "core", and REVERT refuses the modules that
declare MODULE_MIGRATIONS_AUTO: reverting one would drop the tables from
under a module that is running.

The replies come back when the database has answered, which may be after
the command has returned; a migration takes as long as it takes (see
migration_timeout below), and Services go on serving users meanwhile.
Every migration applied or reverted is written to the log and announced by
WALLOPS.

5. The table
------------

    id                     bigint, generated
    version                integer
    module_name            text           "core" for the core's own
    module_migration_name  text           the <name> from the file
    exec_duration          text           "840ms", "3.2s", "4m 11s"
    exec_by                text           the operator's nick, or
                                          "Services" when applied
                                          automatically
    created_at             timestamptz    when it ran

with a unique index on (module_name, version): that is what stops two
operators applying the same migration at the same moment and each
believing they were first.

What is in the table is what is applied.  Services keep no second opinion
anywhere, and reverting a migration deletes its row.

6. How one runs
---------------

Each migration is one transaction -- for OperServ MIGRATION, on a
connection of its own, in a worker thread; for the core's set and the
automatic ones, on the main thread's own connection, since nothing can
happen until they are done:

    BEGIN
    <the whole .sql script>
    INSERT INTO migrations (...) VALUES (...)      -- or DELETE, to revert
    COMMIT

A script that fails leaves neither the change nor the record, so a
recorded migration is one that happened.  The steps of an APPLY or a
REVERT run one at a time, and a failure stops the sequence; the ones
before it stay applied, so that the broken one can be fixed and the
command run again.

The script is the one place where SQL goes to the database as a string
rather than as a prepared statement.  It has to: PostgreSQL does not
prepare DDL, and a script is several statements.  It is safe because the
string is a file the module's author shipped, embedded at build time, and
nothing a user types can reach it.  The row recording it carries an
operator's nick, and that one is bound like every other value.

Every connection's search path is the configured schema, then public, so
unqualified table names land in Services' schema.

The time a migration may take is `migration_timeout' in the database
block: a minute by default, at most an hour.  It is not the five-second
limit of ordinary queries, since an ALTER TABLE on a table with real data
in it takes minutes; there is still a ceiling, because a migration blocked
on a lock forever is one nobody hears about again.

7. Writing one
--------------

1. Add the pair, both halves, same version, same name:

       modules/misc/scores/migrations/v3_add_email.up.sql
       modules/misc/scores/migrations/v3_add_email.down.sql

2. Make the up idempotent where you can (CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS, ADD
   COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS): a half-failed migration then simply runs again.

3. Make the down undo the up, and nothing more: a down that drops the
   column the up added is right; one that drops the whole table the up
   altered is not.

4. Build.  There is nothing to register: the directory is found and
   embedded.

5. Load the module (or restart), OperServ MIGRATION STATUS to see v3
   pending, and OperServ MIGRATION APPLY when the network is ready.

Never edit a migration that has been applied anywhere; add the next one.
An edited migration says one thing to the databases that ran it and
another to those that have not.

For a module that declares MODULE_MIGRATIONS_AUTO, step 5 is the restart
(or the module being loaded again): its new migrations are applied then,
before its init_module() runs.  Its tables are the ones the entity store
reads and writes (readme.database, section 2), so a new column must also
appear in the record's bundle -- and must have a default, since rows
written by the previous version do not have it.

